codex
**VERDICT: The legal-write claim holds, but the termination claim is stale for this checkout. GENCTL accepts all 256 byte values; C040 now suppresses interrupt assertion. Exactly one executable `exit(1)` remains, in `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`.**

**Q1.** The path visible in the permitted files starts at `dev_pcc2_access`, defined by `DEVICE_ACCESS(pcc2)` and registered by `DEVINIT(pcc2)`. The handler calls `memory_readmax64()` to obtain the write value, stores it in GENCTL, and calls `reassert_interrupts(d)` during that same access. [GENCTL handler](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:486)

The helper’s current guard is `d->pcctwo_reg[PCCTWO_GENCTL] & PCC2_C040`. When that is true, it announces once per instance through `fatal()`:

`[ pcc2: C040 (MC68040 EIPL) interrupt delivery not implemented; assertion suppressed  (once) ]`

It then sets `assert = 0`, reaches `INTERRUPT_DEASSERT(d->cpu_irq)`, and returns. There is **no executable `exit(1)` in this helper**. Its comments describe an earlier termination at the top of the function, but that arm and its original diagnostic are absent from the current code. The opening comment claiming this write “still ends the host” contradicts the implementation. [Current C040 arm](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:369)

**Q2.** Yes, in your stated sense of acceptance by the register handler. Its only declining guard is `len != 1 && writeflag == MEM_WRITE`. After that guard, a write performs `d->pcctwo_reg[relative_addr] = idata` without any value check or mask.

Starting with the producer’s domain—a byte—the accepted set is exactly `{0x00, 0x01, …, 0xfe, 0xff}`, cardinality **256**. The accepted values reaching the alleged C040 termination form the **empty set**; all 256 avoid that nonexistent arm.

The C040 partition is also exact: clear-bit values are `8k + r`, with `k = 0…31` and `r ∈ {0,1,2,3}`; set-bit values have the same `k` and `r ∈ {4,5,6,7}`. Each set contains **128** values. The latter set selects suppression, without termination. The header explicitly permits byte accesses; acceptance of every byte pattern does not establish hardware semantics for every reserved bit. [Register definitions and access-width statement](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h:9)

**Q3.** The operative header symbol is `PCC2_C040`, defined as `0x04`. There is also a separate `PCC2_GENCTL_C040` definition with value `0x03`, but this device file never uses it. [Header definitions](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/mvme_pcctworeg.h:59)

The header supplies no explanatory prose for C040. The device’s comment and diagnostic identify it as selection of **MC68040 EIPL interrupt delivery**. That comment describes delivery-pin selection while retaining calculation of the interrupt level; this is the explanation present in the permitted source, without independently consulting its cited hardware manual.

The model stores the bit and exposes it through the ordinary register-read `memcpy()`. Its sole explicit C040 bit test is in `reassert_interrupts()`. That test runs after IPL, pending interrupt status, and vector bookkeeping have been recalculated. It suppresses the modeled CPU interrupt output and preserves that bookkeeping. The adjacent MIEN test independently suppresses assertion when master interrupt enable is clear. Thus C040 is retained and acted upon; the alternate delivery mechanism remains unimplemented. [Interrupt-delivery handling](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:339)

**Q4.** Excluding comments, the file contains exactly **one** `exit(1)`, at line 414. It is the `default` arm of `pcctwo_interrupt_common()`, guarded by an `interrupt->line` that matches none of `PCC2V_SCSI`, `PCC2V_SCC_TX`, or `PCC2V_SCC_RX`. Its diagnostic is `[ pcctwo_interrupt_common: TODO: line = %i ]`, followed by a newline. Both `pcctwo_interrupt_assert()` and `pcctwo_interrupt_deassert()` call this function; either direction encounters the same guard. The exit occurs **before** its call to `reassert_interrupts()`. [Remaining termination](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:385)

For the producer-side enumeration, initialization assigns `templ.line = i` for `i = 0…15`, registering **16** callback identifiers. The three handled identifiers are `0x05` SCSI, `0x0e` SCC_TX, and `0x0f` SCC_RX. The other **13** are `0x00` PPBUSY, `0x01` PPPE, `0x02` PPSELECT, `0x03` PPFAULT, `0x04` PPACK, `0x06` IEFAIL, `0x07` IE, `0x08` TIMER2, `0x09` TIMER1, `0x0a` GPIO, `0x0b` unnamed, `0x0c` SCC_RXE, and `0x0d` SCC_M. [Callback registration](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:792)

Registration establishes those callback identities, **not actual emission by connected peripherals or guest reachability**. The permitted evidence does not establish which unsupported callbacks can actually be invoked. In particular, the local timer producer updates pending counters directly; timer vector values later placed in `cur_int_vec[]` are acknowledgment results, not calls into this switch.

The remaining explicit termination therefore belongs to one unsupported-interrupt-source class, independent of GENCTL values and access widths.

**Q5.** The existing width idiom is `pcc2_width_decline()` followed by `break`: announce once per block per instance using `d->warned`, leave the register untouched, and eventually return success from the access handler. I count **12 guard blocks**, enumerated as GENCTL, VECBASE, PSCALEADJ, T1CTL/T2CTL, T1ICR/T2ICR, SCCERR, SCCICR, SCCTX, SCCRX, SCSIICR, IPL, and MASK. These cover **14 register labels**, because each timer pair shares one block. Their guard is write-only; reads retain the data supplied before the switch, subject to the span check. [Width-decline helper](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:438)

There are **seven blocks** that apply supported semantics and diagnose unsupported value bits without exiting: VECBASE stores `idata & 0xf0`; T1CTL/T2CTL updates the enable/control bits and handles overflow clear, warning on `idata & ~7`; T1ICR/T2ICR updates writable bits through `0x17`, separately handles interrupt clear, and warns on `idata & 0xe0`; SCCICR and SCCTX each store `idata & 0x1f`; SCCRX and SCSIICR each store `idata & 0xdf`. The latter four warn about bits outside their respective masks. These diagnostics are unlatched. [VECBASE precedent](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:497), [timer precedents](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:530), [serial and SCSI precedents](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:598)

The other existing forms are diagnostic-and-ignore for CHIPID/CHIPREV writes, PSCALEADJ byte writes, and writes to `mvme187_iack`; diagnostic-once-and-ignore for IPL byte writes; and diagnostic-once while retaining the returned data for an IPL read reporting zero. The IPL latches are function-static and process-wide. [Read-only register handling](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:478), [PSCALEADJ](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:519), [IPL](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:660), [acknowledgment writes](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:746)

The register-switch default preserves already-served read data and declines writes, announcing once per offset per instance; reads and writes share that offset’s latch. Reads spanning beyond the register array receive zeros with a process-wide once-only diagnostic. The counter/compare write block silently declines an out-of-span copy. Finally, C040 itself now uses a per-instance diagnostic latch plus output suppression, alongside the existing MIEN suppression idiom. [Default handling](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:700), [read-span handling](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:461), [bounded counter writes](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:510)

**Q6.** There are **three enclosing caller functions and ten direct call sites**. `dev_pcc2_access()` supplies eight: GENCTL at line 493; shared T1ICR/T2ICR at 585; SCCICR at 608; SCCTX at 622; SCCRX at 636; SCCRXIACK at 643; SCSIICR at 656; and MASK at 696. All except SCCRXIACK require a byte write. SCCRXIACK has neither a direction guard nor a width guard: an access clears SCCRX interrupt status and calls the helper. [SCCRX acknowledgment arm](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:640)

The other two sites are `pcctwo_interrupt_common()` after a handled interrupt line, and the periodic `dev_pcc2_tick()`. The separately registered `pcc_timer_tick()` does not directly call `reassert_interrupts()`. [Callback and device-tick callers](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pcc2.c:417)

Every listed site consults the stored GENCTL value when it enters the helper, so each can encounter the same C040 suppression without that particular call originating from a GENCTL write. None encounters a C040 termination in this version. The unsupported-line termination described in Q4 occurs upstream and independently.

Within `machine_mvme88k.c`, **MVME187 instantiates PCC2; MVME188 and MVME197 do not**, having TODO-only setup arms. The detector’s chosen values and historical changes cannot be independently verified from the permitted current files. [Machine instantiation](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine_mvme88k.c:83)
